## SuperPowers Operating Rules

**SuperPowers Skills must be applied in every case.**

### Basic Principles

1. **Skill check at conversation start** - Skills live under `.codex/skills/`. If a SuperPowers skill is even 1% applicable, read its `SKILL.md` and follow it.
2. **No skipping skills** - "It's simple" or "Let me read code first" are not valid reasons to skip
3. **Process skills first** - When multiple skills apply: process skills (brainstorming, debugging) -> implementation skills

### Required Skills

| Scenario | Skill Path | Notes |
|----------|------------|-------|
| Before new feature/change | `.codex/skills/superpowers/brainstorming/SKILL.md` | Explore requirements first |
| Multi-step implementation planning | `.codex/skills/superpowers/writing-plans/SKILL.md` | Plan before code |
| Plan execution | `.codex/skills/superpowers/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md` | Subagent-driven by default |
| Bug / test failure investigation | `.codex/skills/superpowers/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md` | Debug before fix |
| Test design before implementation (High/Medium risk) | `.codex/skills/project/test-design/SKILL.md` | Test oracles + falsification items before code (quality policy §4) |
| Testing implementation | `.codex/skills/superpowers/test-driven-development/SKILL.md` | TDD |
| Receiving code review | `.codex/skills/superpowers/receiving-code-review/SKILL.md` | Verify, not blind agree |
| Before completion / merge | `.codex/skills/superpowers/verification-before-completion/SKILL.md` | Evidence-based |
| Post-completion integration | `.codex/skills/superpowers/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md` | Merge/PR/cleanup |
| 2+ independent tasks | `.codex/skills/superpowers/dispatching-parallel-agents/SKILL.md` | Parallel agents for efficiency |
| Feature isolation needed | `.codex/skills/superpowers/using-git-worktrees/SKILL.md` | Worktree isolation |
| Parallel worktree development | `.codex/skills/project/worktree-parallel/SKILL.md` | Worktree placement, ports, env setup, cleanup |
| Before push / merge completion | `.codex/skills/project/self-improvement/SKILL.md` | Apply approved harness improvements before completion |

### Plan Execution Rules

1. **Subagent-driven execution by default** - Execute plans by spawning a fresh subagent per task and reviewing between tasks (see `.codex/skills/superpowers/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md`). Use inline execution (`executing-plans`) only when the user explicitly requests it.
2. **Plan files are not committed** - `docs/superpowers/plans/*.md` must NOT be committed.
3. **Plan file lifecycle** - Kept locally during implementation, deleted after PR merge.

**Loop protection for the implement-review loop** (quality policy §5.5 - cap / stall detection / reduction / escalation):

4. **Review round cap** - Per task, at most 2 rounds of spec-conformance review and at most 2 rounds of quality review. After the cap, only **High** (must-fix) findings block; **Medium** findings go to a follow-up list and the run moves on; Low findings are recorded only. Decide on recorded Medium findings as a batch at the end of the phase.
5. **Stall detection** - Two consecutive rounds with only Minor new findings -> stop reviewing and move to the next task.
6. **Ceremony reduction** - Docs-oriented tasks under 100 diff lines: one combined spec + quality review instead of two. 1-2 line mechanical fixes: the orchestrating session checks the diff itself - spawn no reviewer subagent.
7. **Subagent budget in the plan** - Every implementation plan states an expected subagent-dispatch count (target and cap); escalate to the user when the run is projected to exceed it.
8. **This applies to in-development task reviews only** - The final quality-check gate (Step 4) keeps its own rule that every High/Medium finding is addressed. Never carry the follow-up-list rule into the merge gate.

### Subagent Model Selection

**When spawning subagents, always specify `model` and `model_reasoning_effort` explicitly based on the nature of the task. Do not rely on inheriting the parent session settings.**

**This table takes precedence over any model-selection guidance inside individual skills** (e.g. the "Model Selection" section of `.codex/skills/superpowers/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md`, which assumes a cheapest-model-per-role strategy). When they conflict, follow this table.

| Task Type | Model | Reasoning Effort | Examples |
|-----------|-------|------------------|----------|
| Initial planning / design framing | `gpt-5.6-sol` | `high` | First-pass requirements decomposition, architecture option framing, implementation strategy design |
| Codebase exploration / context gathering | `gpt-5.4-mini` | `medium` | Understanding project structure during planning, read-heavy scans, finding related implementations |
| Simple mechanical search | `gpt-5.4-mini` | `low` | File/symbol lookup, listing usages, collecting file paths |
| Implementation / concrete work | `gpt-5.6-terra` (alternative: `gpt-5.6-sol` with lowered effort) | `medium` | Task-by-task implementation subagents, test writing, refactoring, migrations |
| Spec-conformance review (mechanical diff-vs-spec check) | `gpt-5.4-mini` | `medium` | Checking that a task diff matches the plan/spec text, checklist-style conformance passes |
| Quality / architecture review, quality-check personas | `gpt-5.6-sol` | `high` | 6-persona quality-check review, code review, architecture critique, security analysis, design document review |

Rules:

1. **Planning/design subagents** -> `gpt-5.6-sol` with `high` effort. This is the Codex-side counterpart of Fable in Claude Code.
2. **Exploration subagents** (read-only research during planning) -> `gpt-5.4-mini` with `medium` effort. Speed matters more than depth; results are distilled back to the main session.
3. **Implementation subagents** (task executors in subagent-driven execution) -> `gpt-5.6-terra`, or `gpt-5.6-sol` with `model_reasoning_effort` lowered to `medium`/`low`. Do not spend high-effort frontier models on routine implementation.
4. **Review subagents are split by review type** (quality policy §5.5, model tiering): **spec-conformance review** - mechanically checking a diff against the plan/spec - runs on `gpt-5.4-mini` with `medium` effort. **Quality, architecture and security review, and the quality-check personas** run on `gpt-5.6-sol` with `high` effort, because that review directly gates push/merge. Never downgrade the quality-check personas to a mini model.
5. If reusable, define these as project agents under `.codex/agents/` (e.g. `implementer.toml` with `model = "gpt-5.6-terra"`, `reviewer.toml` with `model = "gpt-5.6-sol"` / `model_reasoning_effort = "high"` / `sandbox_mode = "read-only"`). Otherwise, state the model explicitly in the spawn prompt.
6. When in doubt between two tiers, choose the more capable model.

